  "title": "An unreadable ancestor leaves higher ancestors selectable",
  "file": "src/util/proc.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "During cleanup, the factory cannot inspect its parent but can inspect a higher ancestor carrying the invocation marker. It treats that ancestor as an owned child and can terminate an unrelated runner or supervising process.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun \"$TMPDIR/adjudicate-443-ahf3nxw5/probe.ts\"",
    "output": "F1 Linux: grandparent excluded=false; selected=[424245]\nF1 Darwin: grandparent excluded=false; selected=[424245,424243,424242]"
   },
   "evidence": "The probe bundled the actual HEAD scanners with synthetic filesystem and libproc backends; no real ancestor was signalled. Linux ancestry handles EACCES and other listed errors with `break` at line 57, then returns the incomplete `protectedPids`. Darwin does the same at processes-darwin.ts:125. Both scanners selected the readable, marked synthetic grandparent after its unreadable parent stopped the ancestry walk. Linux selection uses `if (carriesMarker(environment.split(\"\\0\"))) marked.push(Number(name));`; Darwin uses `marked.add(pid)` for a matching environment. proc.ts:245-266 then claims selected processes with readable birth identities and signals them; an ancestor's stable identity does not establish ownership. Base had no caller-ancestry exclusions, so this is an incomplete delivery of the requested protection rather than a newly introduced exposure."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "NOT_REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "No real process can hit this. Every invocation gets a new random marker, and the caller's ancestors started before that marker existed, so none of them can carry it. They also cannot be in the new child's own process group or session.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun $TMPDIR/adj/f1.ts (calls linuxCallerAncestors with a stat reader that throws EACCES for process.ppid and would return a stat row for a grandparent 999001)",
    "output": "ppid 50606 protected [ 1, 50675, 50606 ] grandparent protected? false"
   },
   "evidence": "The walk does stop at an unreadable ancestor (proc.ts:57-59: `if ([\"ENOENT\",\"ESRCH\",\"EACCES\",\"EPERM\"].includes(...)) break;`; processes-darwin.ts:125 does the same), so higher ancestors are left out of the protected set. That part is mechanically true. But nothing outside the protected set can select a real ancestor. (1) The marker is `randomUUID()`, created just before spawn (proc.ts:356-369), and environment only flows down to children, so no earlier ancestor can have `LIMITLESS_INVOCATION=<marker>` or `LIMITLESS_INVOCATION_<marker>=1`. agentEnv keeps only outer markers. (2) The Linux /proc scan and the ps path select by marker only (proc.ts:93-96, 141-147, 191-197). (3) On Darwin, group/session membership needs `leader.pid === group`, where group is the new detached child's pid. Ancestor-chain membership needs a marked parent. Hidden-orphan membership needs a birth time after the invocation and a cwd in the run's directories. An ancestor born before the spawn meets none of these. (4) An ENOENT means the parent exited mid-walk. The caller has then been reparented, so the old grandparent is no longer its ancestor, and it still lacks the marker. On Linux, /proc/<pid>/stat of a same-uid process is not EACCES even with hidepid. The reviewer's probe worked only because it fed the scanners synthetic data in which an ancestor carries the fresh marker, which cannot happen on a real machine."
  }
